 ====
 
+[[super-mapping-inheritance]]
+=== Inheriting mappings from overridden methods
+
+An overriding bean mapping method can inherit the property mappings declared by the methods it overrides by enabling
+`@BeanMapping(inheritSuperMappings = true)`. This includes `@Mapping` declarations supplied through mapping
+composition annotations, but does not implicitly inherit other method-level options such as `@BeanMapping` settings.
+
+.Mapping method inheriting mappings from an overridden method
+====
+[source, java, linenums]
+[subs="verbatim,attributes"]
+----
+public interface BaseCarMapper {
+
+    @Mapping(target = "numberOfSeats", source = "seatCount")
+    Car carDtoToCar(CarDto source);
+}
+
+@Mapper
+public interface CarMapper extends BaseCarMapper {
+
+    @Override
+    @BeanMapping(inheritSuperMappings = true)
+    Car carDtoToCar(CarDto carDto);
+}
+----
+====
+
+A mapping or ignore declared on the overriding method takes precedence for the same target path. Otherwise, the
+mapping from the most specific overridden declaration is used. Mappings for different targets from unrelated parent
+interfaces are combined. If unrelated parent interfaces declare mappings for the same target, the ambiguity must be
+resolved on the overriding method. Mappings with `target = "."` always accumulate.
+
+Override relationships are resolved transitively and through generic supertypes. Qualified source parameter paths are
+rebound to renamed parameters by signature position. Java expressions are copied without modification.
+
 [[inverse-mappings]]
 === Inverse mappings
 
